# Citation metadata for Ankora, in Citation File Format 1.2.0.
# https://citation-file-format.github.io
#
# GitHub reads this file and offers "Cite this repository" on the repository
# page, in APA and BibTeX. Update `version` and `date-released` with each
# tagged release so a published result names the version that produced it.
#
# TODO: add `orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000"` under the author
# once available. It is the only durable identifier that survives a name change
# or a shared surname, and reviewers increasingly expect it.
cff-version: 1.2.0
title: Ankora
message: >-
  If you use Ankora in work you publish, please cite it using this metadata,
  naming the version that produced your results.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Olascoaga
    given-names: Samael
    email: olaskuaga@gmail.com
abstract: >-
  Ankora is a native Windows workbench for molecular docking that keeps the
  scientist in control of every inspection, preparation, execution and review
  decision. It preserves imported structures and compound libraries unchanged,
  requires each chemical decision to be explicit rather than inferred, and
  records the provenance of every transformation. Docking runs against native
  AutoDock Vina or the AutoDock4 scoring family, on CPU or GPU, with immutable
  reusable grid maps and preserved raw engine evidence. Results, redocking
  validation, exact-pose interaction analysis and reproducible campaign bundles
  are recorded per project, and Ankora renders the Methods section a campaign's
  own records support — stating what was not recorded rather than filling the
  gap.
version: 0.1.0
date-released: '2026-08-31'
license: MIT
repository-code: 'https://github.com/Olascoaga/Ankora'
url: 'https://github.com/Olascoaga/Ankora'
keywords:
  - molecular docking
  - virtual screening
  - structure-based drug design
  - reproducibility
  - provenance
  - AutoDock Vina
  - AutoDock4
  - AutoDock-GPU
  - cheminformatics
